e) giving \\\\\\\". That's four characters. But we want three characters: \\\", which is backslash double-quote. To get that, we should not escape the backslash that is already part of an escape sequence? But the backslash in S is a literal backslash, so it must be escaped. However, when we have a literal backslash followed by double-quote, that's exactly the JSON escape sequence for a double quote. So we want the resulting string to contain a single backslash and a double quote. That is represented in JSON text as \\\". But to get that, we need to write \\\" in the JSON text. But our S already has backslash and double quote. If we escape the backslash, we get \\\\, and then the double-quote we escape as \\\", resulting in \\\\\\\", which is two backslashes and a double quote (parsing: escaped backslash -> backslash, then escaped double quote -> double quote, so result is backslash double-quote? Wait, parsing: JSON parser sees \\\\, interprets as a single backslash, then sees \\\", interprets as double quote, so result is backslash double-quote. That's correct! So \\\\\\\" yields a backslash followed by a double quote in the parsed string. So that's fine. But we had S containing backslash and double quote, which we want to turn into the same. So the replace works.\n\nBut let's verify: Starting from S with backslash-doublequote. We replace \\ with \\\\, so becomes \\\\ (two char), then we replace \" with \\\", so we add another backslash before the double-quote: the original double-quote becomes \\\". So after both, we have \\\\ + \\\" = \\\\\\\". That is four characters: \\ \\ \\ \". The JSON parser will see \\\\ as escaped backslash (produces one backslash), then \\\" as escaped double-quote (produces double-quote). So final result is backslash double-quote. Good.\n\nSimilarly for \\n: S has backslash and n. Replace \\ with \\\\, so becomes \\\\n. Then replace \" (no effect). So we get \\\\n. JSON parser sees \\\\ as backslash, then n as n, so result is backslash n